### My answer to the prompt

A reliable implementation needs to measure the text without a line limit, then show a collapsed version only if that measurement finds more than one line. For an inline “See more” link, the collapsed text also needs to leave room for the link; simply appending it after the full text can cause React Native’s one-line truncation to hide the link.

For example, the measurement step can use an off-screen `Text` with `onTextLayout` and no `numberOfLines`:

```jsx
<Text
  style={[textStyle, styles.measurement]}
  onTextLayout={({ nativeEvent }) => {
    setHasOverflow(nativeEvent.lines.length > 1);
  }}
>
  {text}
</Text>
```

Then render the visible text with `numberOfLines={expanded ? undefined : 1}` and show an accessible “See more” control only when `hasOverflow` is true. To guarantee that the control remains visible at the end of the first line, the collapsed text must be shortened to make room for it; this needs to be tested with the app’s supported platforms and text styles.

### Evaluation

Assistant A is closer to the requested behavior: it uses React Native components, provides a usable component shape, and keeps “See more” as a nested, pressable `Text`. However, its overflow detection is unreliable because it checks `onTextLayout` on the same text already limited to one line. Also, appending “See more” after the full text does not guarantee that the link will survive the one-line truncation. So it is not production-ready as claimed.

Assistant B has more fundamental problems. It calls `measureInLayout()` synchronously during render and treats the result as a width object; React Native’s measurement APIs do not work that way. Its ref may also be unset on the initial render, and it never updates state after measuring. With the default `maxWidth` of `0`, truncation is disabled altogether. The “See more” button is laid out beside the text rather than inline at the end of its line, and the usage example includes `<h2>`, which is not a React Native component. Its claims of robust detection and production readiness are therefore inaccurate.

Neither answer provides a correct production-ready implementation of the requested inline behavior. Assistant A is nevertheless more relevant and closer to a working starting point; Assistant B’s measurement and layout logic are substantially broken.

My final verdict is: [[A>>B]]

## My answer to the prompt

A reliable implementation should measure the full text at the available width, then show a one-line preview and only display the action when the text actually overflows. Here is a TypeScript version with an adjacent “See more” action; placing it beside the text rather than appending it after the full string ensures the action remains visible.

```tsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import {
  LayoutChangeEvent,
  StyleProp,
  StyleSheet,
  Text,
  TextStyle,
  Pressable,
  View,
  ViewStyle,
} from 'react-native';

type Props = {
  text: string;
  textStyle?: StyleProp<TextStyle>;
  containerStyle?: StyleProp<ViewStyle>;
  seeMoreLabel?: string;
  showLessLabel?: string;
};

export default function ExpandableText({
  text,
  textStyle,
  containerStyle,
  seeMoreLabel = 'See more',
  showLessLabel = 'Show less',
}: Props) {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);
  const [overflows, setOverflows] = useState(false);
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    setOverflows(false);
    setExpanded(false);
  }, [text, width]);

  const onContainerLayout = (event: LayoutChangeEvent) => {
    const nextWidth = event.nativeEvent.layout.width;
    if (nextWidth !== width) setWidth(nextWidth);
  };

  return (
    <View style={containerStyle} onLayout={onContainerLayout}>
      {width > 0 && (
        <Text
          accessible={false}
          pointerEvents="none"
          onTextLayout={({ nativeEvent }) => {
            const next = nativeEvent.lines.length > 1;
            setOverflows(current => (current === next ? current : next));
          }}
          style={[styles.text, textStyle, styles.measurement, { width }]}
        >
          {text}
        </Text>
      )}

      {expanded ? (
        <View>
          <Text style={[styles.text, textStyle]}>{text}</Text>
          <Pressable
            accessibilityRole="button"
            accessibilityLabel={showLessLabel}
            accessibilityState={{ expanded: true }}
            onPress={() => setExpanded(false)}
            hitSlop={8}
          >
            <Text style={styles.action}>{showLessLabel}</Text>
          </Pressable>
        </View>
      ) : (
        <View style={styles.previewRow}>
          <Text style={[styles.text, styles.preview, textStyle]} numberOfLines={1}>
            {text}
          </Text>
          {overflows && (
            <Pressable
              accessibilityRole="button"
              accessibilityLabel={seeMoreLabel}
              accessibilityState={{ expanded: false }}
              onPress={() => setExpanded(true)}
              hitSlop={8}
            >
              <Text style={styles.action}>{seeMoreLabel}</Text>
            </Pressable>
          )}
        </View>
      )}
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  text: {
    fontSize: 16,
    color: '#333',
  },
  measurement: {
    position: 'absolute',
    opacity: 0,
  },
  previewRow: {
    flexDirection: 'row',
    alignItems: 'center',
  },
  preview: {
    flex: 1,
    flexShrink: 1,
  },
  action: {
    color: '#007AFF',
    marginLeft: 6,
  },
});
```

The preview uses a separate adjacent action so it cannot be clipped behind the end of the text. If the action must be *part of the same text line*, the preview text needs to be measured and shortened to leave room for the action; simply appending a nested `Text` after the full string may cause “See more” itself to be truncated.

## Evaluation

Assistant A is not a functional implementation. `measureInLayout()` is not a standard React Native `Text` measurement method, and the measurement is attempted during render rather than after layout. More importantly, `maxWidth` defaults to `0`, which makes `doesTextExceedWidth()` return `false`; and even when truncation is detected, `isExpanded` is never used to change `numberOfLines` or otherwise reveal the full text. Thus, pressing its button does not expand the text. The usage example also includes `<h2>`, which is not a React Native component. Several claims of production readiness are therefore misleading.

Assistant B is also not production-ready. It has a plausible expansion state and uses `onTextLayout`, so it is closer to a working approach. However, it appends “See more” *after the entire original string* while also limiting the text to one line. The trailing action may be clipped and not visible, and the code does not shorten the displayed text to reserve space for it. Its overflow detection may also be unreliable when measuring text already constrained by `numberOfLines={1}`. It does correctly remove that limit after expansion, which is a meaningful improvement over A.

Neither answer fully solves the inline-label layout problem or adequately handles changes in available width and text. B is substantially better because it at least provides a plausible measurement-and-expansion approach; A’s core logic does not work. My final verdict is: [[B>>A]]

